---
Invoke an agent one-shot

Usage: tau run [OPTIONS] <AGENT_ID> [PROMPT]

Arguments:
  <AGENT_ID>
          Identifier of the agent to invoke

  [PROMPT]
          Prompt for the agent. If omitted, read from stdin

Options:
      --max-turns <MAX_TURNS>
          Override RunOptions::max_turns

      --dry-run
          Validate setup without invoking the LLM

      --no-install
          Skip auto-install of missing requires.tools dependencies. If anything would need fetching, exit 2 with copy-pasteable `tau install <url>` hints instead

      --stream
          Stream events as they arrive. Text deltas appear inline on stdout; tool annotations on stderr. With --json, emits one JSON object per event line (text_delta / tool_call_started / tool_call_completed / turn_completed / run_completed)

      --max-total-tokens <TOKENS>
          Maximum cumulative input + output tokens across all agents spawned in the run. When the budget is exceeded, the run aborts with `RunStatus::Aborted`. Unset = no limit

      --max-total-duration-secs <SECS>
          Maximum wall-clock duration of the run, in seconds. When the budget is exceeded, the run aborts with `RunStatus::Aborted`. Unset = no limit

      --max-total-agents <N>
          Maximum number of agents that may be spawned across the run (root + descendants). When exceeded, the run aborts with `RunStatus::Aborted`. Unset = no limit

      --bundle <PATH>
          Run from a pre-built bundle, verifying the cwd matches it first. The bundle must target this host triple and the project must be installed at the exact tree hashes recorded in the bundle, or the run is refused (Phase 2 §C.3)

      --resume <RUN_ID>
          Resume a durable run by its run id (ADR-0053). The entry agent must declare `[durable]`; the run rehydrates the latest checkpoint under `.tau/runs/<RUN_ID>/` and re-enters at the next turn, so already committed turns are not re-billed.
          
          On the pipeline path (EPIC 4.3), `--resume <RUN_ID>` combined with `--signal <NAME>` resumes a pipeline paused at a top-level `Suspend` step instead: the run rehydrates its `OutputStore` snapshot from `.tau/runs/<RUN_ID>/suspend.json` and continues after the `Suspend` step.

      --signal <NAME>
          Signal name to resume a suspended pipeline run (with `--resume`)

      --allow-ungoverned
          Authorize running a project/bundle with no `[allow]` ceiling. On the dev path a missing `[allow]` is otherwise a hard error (GOV000); on the `--bundle` path this is required to run a bundle built `ungoverned`

      --no-governance
          Dev-run a project that HAS an `[allow]` ceiling without enforcing it. No effect on `--bundle` runs (the bundle's verdict is already sealed)

  -v, --verbose...
          Verbosity level. Repeat for more (-v: DEBUG, -vv: TRACE)

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-error output (sets tracing to WARN)

      --debug
          Show full error chain + DEBUG-level tracing on failures

      --color <COLOR>
          Color mode for terminal output

          Possible values:
          - always: Always emit ANSI color codes
          - auto:   Auto-detect based on terminal capabilities
          - never:  Never emit color codes
          
          [default: auto]

      --json
          Emit structured JSON output instead of human-readable. Only supported on `install`, `list`, `run`, `init`

      --record-protocol <PATH>
          Mirror every plugin protocol frame to a JSONL file at this path.
          
          Useful for debugging plugin behavior and replaying via `tau plugin protocol decode <path>`. The recording is best-effort per spec §7.8: write/open failures are logged but never abort the invocation.

      --no-sandbox
          Disable sandboxing entirely for this invocation (shorthand for `--sandbox passthrough`). For development and one-off debugging. Auditable in shell history

      --sandbox <SANDBOX>
          Force a specific sandbox adapter kind, overriding the resolver. `--no-sandbox` is shorthand for `--sandbox passthrough`. Conflicts with `--no-sandbox` if both set

          Possible values:
          - native:      Linux landlock + seccomp + namespaces
          - container:   Docker / Podman shell-out
          - passthrough: No isolation (explicit opt-out)

      --log-non-blocking
          Write logs through a non-blocking MPSC channel (high-throughput). Requires `--log-file`. Env: `TAU_LOG_NON_BLOCKING=1`
          
          [env: TAU_LOG_NON_BLOCKING=]

      --log-file <PATH>
          Write logs to the given file instead of stderr. Required when `--log-non-blocking` is set. Env: `TAU_LOG_FILE`
          
          [env: TAU_LOG_FILE=]

      --log-rotation <LOG_ROTATION>
          Log file rotation policy. Ignored unless `--log-file` is set. Env: `TAU_LOG_ROTATION`

          Possible values:
          - never:  No rotation — append forever to a single file
          - daily:  Roll over each day at UTC midnight (filename gains a date suffix)
          - hourly: Roll over each hour (filename gains a date+hour suffix)
          
          [env: TAU_LOG_ROTATION=]
          [default: never]

      --otlp-endpoint <URL>
          Export traces over OTLP/gRPC to this endpoint, e.g. `https://otel.example.com:4317`. Falls back to `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` env var. Auth + tenant headers come from `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS` (comma-separated `key=value` pairs). Requires the `otlp` cargo feature (on by default)
          
          [env: OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
